# %% [markdown] id="zo32sLNBsYZm"
# # **Principal component analysis (PCA):**
# is a method of reducing the dimensionality of data and is used to improve data visualization and speed up machine learning model training.

# %% [markdown] id="Ujd9qyEFs98X"
# ## **Important Libraries**

# %% id="M39dhYJNtI0t"
import pandas as pd
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt


# %% [markdown] id="JNtMgY0psRNB"
# # **PCA for Data Visualization**

# %% [markdown] id="AX4LRaLHs0XI"
# ## **1-LOAD THE IRIS DATA SET**
#
# ![image.png](data:image/png;base64,)

# %% id="Of0MUwgEsHvH"
url = "https://archive.ics.uci.edu/ml/machine-learning-databases/iris/iris.data"

# load dataset into Pandas DataFrame
#df = pd.read_csv(url, names=['sepal length','sepal width','petal length','petal width','target'])
df = pd.read_csv("iris.csv")

# %% colab={"base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/", "height": 0} id="e0w7tGcBtMLb" outputId="4533d6c0-8bea-46d6-ad27-17dcff8d6dc1"
df.head()


# %% colab={"base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/"} id="yXvpRzSGtSEr" outputId="b1fada0d-2b5e-4b9a-b1e9-dc6ff6775716"
df.shape

# %% colab={"base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/"} id="RLzp9fg62iPs" outputId="94272b33-a573-4ecb-f350-6795f9584d93"
df.columns

# %% [markdown] id="CI1iluF80anR"
# ## **2-STANDARDIZE THE DATA**
#
# PCA is affected by scale, so you need to scale the features in your data before applying PCA. Use StandardScaler to help you standardize the data set’s features onto unit scale (mean = 0 and variance = 1), which is a requirement for the optimal performance of many machine learning algorithms. If you don’t scale your data, it can have a negative effect on your algorithm.
#
#

# %% id="FYnSdRsYznTL"
from sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler

# Separating out the features
X=df.drop('target', axis=1)

# Separating out the target
y= df['target']


# Standardizing the features
X = StandardScaler().fit_transform(X)

# %% id="1Y5ndcD_z8mC"
X_df=pd.DataFrame(X,columns=['sepal length', 'sepal width', 'petal length', 'petal width'])

# %% colab={"base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/", "height": 0} id="TXbsvnxa2Y1_" outputId="602ed97a-358a-4854-ddd1-0b13f68a1329"
df.drop('target',axis=1).head()

# %% colab={"base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/", "height": 0} id="eRloU1Yn1ixD" outputId="79834c77-22bc-4c91-91c5-c72131db795a"
X_df.head()

# %% [markdown] id="60en9IWg4nIx"
# ## **3-PCA PROJECTION TO 2D**

# %% id="pF0s3fpA3zp_"
from sklearn.decomposition import PCA

pca = PCA(n_components=2)
principalComponents = pca.fit_transform(X)

principalDf = pd.DataFrame(data = principalComponents
             , columns = ['principal component 1', 'principal component 2'])

# %% colab={"base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/", "height": 0} id="dYrbtFaA44ae" outputId="b1c2d514-f3ac-4b82-ea4b-0743a19a7552"
principalDf.head()

# %% colab={"base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/", "height": 0} id="qQ6N6Q175A2V" outputId="6fe0db1a-51b9-43d1-8cc1-c308c62313f1"
finalDf = pd.concat([principalDf, df[['target']]], axis = 1)
finalDf.head()

# %% [markdown] id="mw0csy3c5Xkk"
# ## **4-VISUALIZE 2D PROJECTION**

# %% colab={"base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/", "height": 732} id="t-9I01G35Pqr" outputId="c277b362-93c7-40d6-db27-fd068e00c332"
fig = plt.figure(figsize = (8,8))
ax = fig.add_subplot(1,1,1)
ax.set_xlabel('Principal Component 1', fontsize = 15)
ax.set_ylabel('Principal Component 2', fontsize = 15)
ax.set_title('2 component PCA', fontsize = 20)

targets = ['Iris-setosa', 'Iris-versicolor', 'Iris-virginica']
colors = ['r', 'g', 'b']
for target, color in zip(targets,colors):
    indicesToKeep = finalDf['target'] == target
    ax.scatter(finalDf.loc[indicesToKeep, 'principal component 1']
               , finalDf.loc[indicesToKeep, 'principal component 2']
               , c = color
               , s = 50)
ax.legend(targets)
ax.grid()

# %% colab={"base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/"} id="1n5hqUNXsgTo" outputId="e7aec2ab-97a8-406a-9034-475568c10a90"
pca.explained_variance_ratio_

# %% [markdown] id="c1ex9AbduHrO"
# ### **explained_variance_ratio_  tells us:**
# 1. The first principal component explains 72.77% of the total variance in the
# original data.
# 2. The second principal component explains 23.03% of the total variance.
# 3. Together, these two principal components explain 95.80% of the total variance in the original four-dimensional dataset.

# %% [markdown] id="1DpuBQ9qvAVp"
# **Note:**
# Understanding the explained variance ratio is crucial for determining the trade-off between dimensionality reduction and information loss. It helps in deciding how many principal components to retain based on the amount of variance they capture.

# %% [markdown] id="Ve9DvCGDvM2o"
# # **PCA to Speed-Up Machine Learning Algorithms**

# %% [markdown] id="iInnbxOgwZ4H"
# ## **Load the data**

# %% [markdown] id="5mwCUDPFwQzC"
# **The MNIST database** of handwritten digits is more suitable, as it has 784 (28*28)feature columns (784 dimensions), a training set of 60,000 examples and a test set of 10,000 examples.
#
# ![image.png](data:image/png;base64,)

# %% colab={"base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/"} id="_kpLKov0wP9S" outputId="584e1e55-3e87-42a1-d34e-697ae367a7c6"
from sklearn.datasets import fetch_openml
mnist = fetch_openml('mnist_784')

# %% colab={"base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/", "height": 254} id="AhttXfL4_J0R" outputId="18a50017-7e10-43b1-a99c-a64db0f116fd"
# Convert feature matrix and target labels to Pandas DataFrame
df_features = pd.DataFrame(mnist['data'], columns=mnist['feature_names'])
df_target = pd.DataFrame({'target': mnist['target']})

# Display first few rows of the DataFrames
print("Features DataFrame:")
df_features.head()


# %% colab={"base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/", "height": 242} id="s3HilIiWDvgi" outputId="1ccdc277-3427-47d3-9b63-bcc980da5c50"
print("\nTarget DataFrame:")
df_target.head()


# %% colab={"base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/", "height": 564} id="uLxK3MdTD7vY" outputId="8be7aed4-1d64-4333-91b0-988d5da2e89c"
# Count occurrences of each class and create a DataFrame
class_counts = pd.DataFrame(df_target.value_counts().reset_index())
class_counts.columns = ['Digit', 'Count']
class_counts = class_counts.sort_values('Digit')

# Plotting the distribution of classes
plt.figure(figsize=(8, 6))
plt.bar(class_counts['Digit'], class_counts['Count'], color='skyblue')
plt.xlabel('Digit')
plt.ylabel('Count')
plt.title('Distribution of Classes in MNIST Dataset')
plt.xticks(class_counts['Digit'])
plt.grid(axis='y')
plt.show()

# %% colab={"base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/", "height": 363} id="8_Odgl41H6m7" outputId="d874cebb-60e7-4c1f-91db-c69238aa449a"
class_counts

# %% [markdown] id="xf6pyb4c_GEt"
# ## **SPLIT DATA INTO TRAINING AND TEST SETS**

# %% id="gW46ZuhlsjT9"
from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split

# test_size: what proportion of original data is used for test set
train_img, test_img, train_lbl, test_lbl = train_test_split( mnist.data, mnist.target, test_size=1/7.0, random_state=0)

# %% [markdown] id="ZTpIFbFJbV93"
# # **STANDARDIZE THE DATA**

# %% id="WRM2cBE-absH"
from sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler
scaler = StandardScaler()

# Fit on training set only.
scaler.fit(train_img)

# Apply transform to both the training set and the test set.
train_img = scaler.transform(train_img)
test_img = scaler.transform(test_img)

# %% [markdown] id="LOc6lma-wVCw"
# ## **IMPORT AND APPLY PCA**

# %% id="hZEuqwgusVcV"
from sklearn.decomposition import PCA

# Make an instance of the Model
pca = PCA(.95)

# %% colab={"base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/", "height": 74} id="iGzf3rFswbtI" outputId="2cf6a959-0e90-4f6b-f562-dd259df49c9d"
pca.fit(train_img)

# %% [markdown] id="Af33nepcwwLU"
# ## **APPLY THE MAPPING (TRANSFORM) TO THE TRAINING SET AND THE TEST SET.**

# %% id="dm-KDNutweRG"
train_img_pca = pca.transform(train_img)
test_img_pca = pca.transform(test_img)

# %%
train_img.shape

# %%
train_img_pca.shape

# %% [markdown] id="zdD8rBcNxRTG"
# ## **APPLY LOGISTIC REGRESSION TO THE TRANSFORMED DATA**

# %% id="mXkML3pHw1m9"
from sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression

# %% id="3hkQCnSEYStw"

# %% id="TQIE_Xs0xdh4"
# all parameters not specified are set to their defaults
# default solver is incredibly slow which is why it was changed to 'lbfgs'
logisticRegr = LogisticRegression()
logisticRegr_pca = LogisticRegression()

# %% colab={"base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/", "height": 366} id="s_uU5MnyxnWu" outputId="853f4895-174d-446a-9cbb-34ab87fcdd49"
#Train the model on the data, storing the information learned from the data
logisticRegr.fit(train_img, train_lbl)

# %%
logisticRegr_pca.fit(train_img_pca, train_lbl)

# %% colab={"base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/"} id="61OD2yU5yb6v" outputId="497dec68-258c-4f48-d998-e5c281d4de75"
# Predict for One Observation (image)
logisticRegr.predict(test_img[0:10])
logisticRegr_pca.predict(test_img_pca[0:10])

# %% [markdown] id="U-c4i0EHzScE"
# ## **MEASURING MODEL PERFORMANCE**

# %% colab={"base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/"} id="Jvma83Jqygh-" outputId="fa2af73a-4f89-4db6-960b-26e258e45c26"
logisticRegr.score(test_img, test_lbl)

# %% colab={"base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/"} id="XHQtLD_PykKl" outputId="a5c3ffa3-adfc-4b2c-9b23-581e914a3ae0"
logisticRegr_pca.score(test_img_pca, test_lbl)

# %% id="eUCes6zbZHVG"
